.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 
The camera: finding one, opening it, and showing what it sees\&. 

\fBPart of the host facing interface\&.\fP A host binds to \fBschultz_api\&.h\fP, which includes this header\&.
.PP
A camera is the other direction from \fBschultz_video\&.h\fP\&. That plays a film somebody else made; this one takes pictures as they happen\&.
.PP
Three things are worth knowing before reading the calls\&.
.PP
\fBA camera is hardware, and may not be there\&.\fP Listing on a machine with none succeeds and reports none\&. That is not an error and a host should not treat it as one: the right answer is to say so and carry on\&.
.PP
\fBOpening one is not the same as being allowed to use it\&.\fP On phones, and increasingly on desktops, the operating system asks the person first, and the answer can take seconds or minutes\&. So opening always succeeds or fails immediately on whether the device exists, and permission is a separate question asked with schultz_camera_permission until it stops saying waiting\&. Until it says allowed, no pictures arrive\&.
.PP
\fBThe pictures come back ready to draw\&.\fP A camera's own format is usually Motion JPEG or one of the packed YUV layouts, and none of them is what a screen wants\&. That conversion happens below this interface, so a frame is always premultiplied ARGB at the size that was asked for\&.
.PP
The short way to show one on screen is the preview node at the bottom of this header, which does all of the above on the host's behalf\&. 
.PP
Definition in file \fBschultz_camera\&.h\fP\&.
